  "worked_solution": "Using the constant multiple, the addition rule for limits, and the fact that both functions are continuous, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 1}[2f(t)+g(t)] & ={lim}_{t\\to 1}2f(t)+{lim}_{t\\to 1}g(t) \\\\  & =2{lim}_{t\\to 1}f(t)+{lim}_{t\\to 1}g(t) \\\\  & =2f(1)+g(1) \\\\  & =2{{\u27e80,1,-2\u27e9}_{\u23df}}_{f(1)}+g(1) \\\\  & =\u27e80,2,-4\u27e9+g(1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, we are told that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{t\\to 1}[2f(t)+g(t)]=\u27e8-1,4,2\u27e9.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following equation which we can solve for $g(1):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 1}[2f(t)+g(t)] & =\u27e8-1,4,2\u27e9 \\\\ \u27e80,2,-4\u27e9+g(1) & =\u27e8-1,4,2\u27e9 \\\\ g(1) & =\u27e8-1,4,2\u27e9-\u27e80,2,-4\u27e9 \\\\ g(1) & =\u27e8-1,2,6\u27e9\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
